            <ArticleTitle>The impact of warming on pain and wound healing after hernia surgery: a preliminary study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>104-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine whether the application of warmth after hernia surgery reduces pain and aids wound healing.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Forty-five patients were recruited and randomised to receive either no warming;two hours of warming immediately after surgery; or seven days of warming.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Postoperative pain scores were significantly lower after two hours of warming immediately after surgery compared with the non-warmed patients (p&lt;0.05). Pain scores were lower on each of the first seven postoperative days in the warmed groups compared with the non-warmed subjects. Patients in the warmed groups also had lower ASEPSIS wound scores.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Warming may allow wounds to heal with fewer complications. In addition, warming for only two hours immediately after surgery may provide similar benefits to seven days of warming.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
